^chapter
^title
State Management
^title/

^section
^title
Property Wrappers
^title/

^paragraph
SwiftUI uses property wrappers to manage state. The most common are listed below.
^paragraph/

^code_block
@State var count = 0
@Binding var isPresented: Bool
@Observable class UserModel {
    var name: String = ""
}
@Environment(\.dismiss) var dismiss
@MainActor func updateUI() {
    // runs on main thread
}
^code_block/

^paragraph
All property wrappers beginning with @ are Swift language features. They are not Springwater tags because they do not appear as the only content on a trimmed line matching the ^name pattern.
^paragraph/
^section/

^section
^title
Observable Pattern
^title/

^paragraph
The modern approach uses @Observable instead of ObservableObject.
^paragraph/

^code_block
@Observable
class ViewModel {
    var items: [Item] = []
    var isLoading = false

    func fetch() async {
        isLoading = true
        items = await service.load()
        isLoading = false
    }
}
^code_block/

^note
Notice that @Observable, @State, and all other Swift annotations appear safely as content. The ^ tag character has no collision with any programming language's annotation syntax.
^note/
^section/
^chapter/
